"""50 lesson topics for the new Critical Thinking practical-skills pathway.

Tuple field order matches the TOPICS lists in
generate_practical_skills_expansion.py:

    (title, description, materials, steps, quiz_q, quiz_a, pro_tip)

Topics are ordered roughly beginner -> advanced; the generator auto-splits
the list into thirds for difficulty levels.
"""

TOPICS: list[tuple] = [
    # ---------------- Foundations ----------------
    ("What Is Critical Thinking?", "Critical thinking is the disciplined process of actively analyzing, evaluating, and synthesizing information rather than passively accepting it. It involves questioning assumptions, weighing evidence, and being willing to change your mind when the facts warrant it. This lesson introduces what critical thinking is, what it is not, and why it matters in everyday decisions.", ["Notebook", "Pen"], ["Write down a belief you hold and ask yourself why you believe it", "List the evidence you actually have for that belief, separating it from things you merely assume", "Identify one question that, if answered, would strengthen or weaken the belief", "Reflect on how it feels to examine your own thinking; this metacognition is the core habit of critical thinking"], "What distinguishes critical thinking from simply having opinions?", "Critical thinking involves actively evaluating evidence and reasoning behind claims, while opinions can be held without any examination of their basis", "Critical thinking is a skill built through practice, not a fixed trait; treat every disagreement or news story as a chance to exercise it."),
    ("Facts, Opinions, and Claims", "A fact can be verified or falsified with evidence, an opinion expresses a preference or value judgment, and a claim is any assertion that may or may not be supported. Confusing these categories is one of the most common sources of muddled thinking and unproductive argument. This lesson teaches you to sort statements into these categories quickly and accurately.", ["Newspaper article or news website", "Notebook", "Pen"], ["Pick a short opinion piece or news article and copy out five statements from it", "Label each statement as a verifiable fact, an opinion or value judgment, or an unsupported claim", "For each factual claim, note what evidence would confirm or refute it", "Notice how many statements presented confidently are actually opinions or unverified claims"], "What makes a statement a fact rather than an opinion?", "A fact can in principle be verified or falsified with evidence, whereas an opinion expresses a preference or value judgment that cannot be settled by evidence alone", "When someone says 'it's a fact that...', ask yourself what specific evidence could verify or falsify it; if nothing could, it isn't a factual claim."),
    ("Asking Better Questions", "Good critical thinkers are defined less by their answers and more by the quality of their questions. Socratic questioning, a method traced back to the philosopher Socrates, uses probing questions about clarity, evidence, assumptions, and implications to dig beneath surface-level claims. This lesson teaches a practical toolkit of question types you can apply to any claim.", ["Notebook", "Pen"], ["Take any claim you encountered today and ask a clarifying question: 'What exactly do you mean by...?'", "Ask an evidence question: 'How do we know this is true? What is it based on?'", "Ask an assumption question: 'What would have to be true for this claim to hold?'", "Ask an implication question: 'If this is true, what follows from it?'", "Practice asking these four question types until they become automatic when you hear a strong claim"], "What are the four main categories of Socratic questions covered in this lesson?", "Questions about clarity (what do you mean), evidence (how do we know), assumptions (what must be true), and implications (what follows if true)", "In conversation, 'What makes you say that?' is a polite, powerful question that invites reasoning without triggering defensiveness."),
    ("Intellectual Humility", "Intellectual humility is the recognition that your knowledge is limited, your beliefs could be wrong, and being corrected is a gain rather than a loss. Research in psychology links intellectual humility with better learning, more accurate beliefs, and more productive disagreements. This lesson covers practical habits for holding beliefs with appropriate confidence.", ["Notebook", "Pen"], ["Write down three things you were confidently wrong about in the past", "For each, note what evidence eventually changed your mind and how long you resisted it", "Practice attaching confidence levels to your beliefs, such as 'I'm about 70% sure' instead of 'definitely'", "When you next feel certain in a discussion, deliberately ask 'What would change my mind?' and take the answer seriously"], "Why is intellectual humility considered a strength rather than a weakness in critical thinking?", "Because recognizing the limits of your knowledge lets you update beliefs when evidence warrants, leading to more accurate views over time, whereas overconfidence locks in errors", "Saying 'I don't know' or 'I was wrong' out loud gets easier with practice and instantly raises the quality of any discussion."),
    ("Deduction vs. Induction", "Deductive reasoning moves from general premises to a conclusion that must be true if the premises are true, while inductive reasoning moves from specific observations to a general conclusion that is probable but not guaranteed. Knowing which type of reasoning is being used tells you how much confidence a conclusion deserves. This lesson teaches you to identify and use both forms correctly.", ["Notebook", "Pen"], ["Study a classic deduction: 'All humans are mortal; Socrates is human; therefore Socrates is mortal' and note how the conclusion is guaranteed by the premises", "Study a classic induction: 'Every swan I have seen is white; therefore all swans are white' and note how the conclusion goes beyond the evidence", "Write two deductive and two inductive arguments of your own", "For each inductive argument, describe what new observation could overturn its conclusion"], "What is the key difference between a deductive and an inductive argument?", "A valid deductive argument guarantees its conclusion if the premises are true, while an inductive argument only makes its conclusion probable, since it generalizes beyond the observed evidence", "Most everyday and scientific reasoning is inductive, so hold those conclusions as probable rather than certain, and stay alert for the black swan."),
    ("Validity vs. Soundness", "In logic, an argument is valid when its conclusion follows necessarily from its premises, and sound when it is valid and its premises are actually true. An argument can be perfectly valid yet completely wrong because it starts from a false premise. This lesson teaches this crucial distinction so you can pinpoint exactly where an argument fails.", ["Notebook", "Pen"], ["Analyze this valid but unsound argument: 'All fish can fly; a salmon is a fish; therefore salmon can fly' and identify the false premise", "Analyze an invalid argument with true premises: 'Some birds can fly; penguins are birds; therefore penguins can fly' and identify the logical gap", "Write one valid-and-sound, one valid-but-unsound, and one invalid argument of your own", "When evaluating any real argument, check the logic first, then check the truth of each premise separately"], "Can an argument be valid but still have a false conclusion?", "Yes; a valid argument guarantees the conclusion only if the premises are true, so a valid argument with at least one false premise (unsound) can have a false conclusion", "When you disagree with an argument, locate exactly where it fails: is the logic broken, or is one of the premises false? Naming which makes your objection far stronger."),
    ("Necessary vs. Sufficient Conditions", "A necessary condition must be present for something to occur, while a sufficient condition guarantees it occurs; being a mammal is necessary for being a dog but not sufficient, and being a dog is sufficient for being a mammal but not necessary. Confusing these two is behind many everyday reasoning errors, from health claims to legal and policy arguments. This lesson trains you to keep them straight.", ["Notebook", "Pen"], ["Learn the definitions: necessary means 'required for', sufficient means 'guarantees'", "Analyze examples: oxygen is necessary for fire but not sufficient; decapitation is sufficient for death but not necessary", "Rewrite conditional claims both ways: 'If it rains, the ground gets wet' means rain is sufficient for wet ground, and wet ground is necessary evidence of... nothing certain, since sprinklers exist", "Find a real claim in the news and identify whether the stated condition is being treated as necessary, sufficient, both, or neither"], "Is water necessary or sufficient for a plant to grow?", "Necessary but not sufficient; a plant cannot grow without water, but water alone does not guarantee growth since light and nutrients are also required", "When someone argues 'X happened without Y, so Y doesn't matter', check whether Y was ever claimed to be necessary rather than merely contributing."),

    # ---------------- Argument analysis ----------------
    ("Identifying Premises and Conclusions", "Every argument has a conclusion (the claim being argued for) and one or more premises (the reasons offered in support). Indicator words like 'because', 'since', and 'therefore' help locate these parts, but real-world arguments often bury or imply them. This lesson teaches you to dissect any argument into its working parts.", ["Newspaper opinion piece or online essay", "Highlighter", "Notebook"], ["Learn conclusion indicators (therefore, thus, so, hence) and premise indicators (because, since, given that, as shown by)", "Take a short opinion piece and highlight the main conclusion in one color", "Highlight each premise offered in support in another color", "Rewrite the argument in standard form: numbered premises followed by the conclusion", "Check whether anything highlighted is neither premise nor conclusion, such as background, rhetoric, or filler"], "Which words typically signal that a conclusion is coming in an argument?", "Words like therefore, thus, so, hence, and consequently typically introduce conclusions", "If you can't find the conclusion, ask 'What is this person ultimately trying to get me to accept?'; everything else is either support for it or noise."),
    ("Argument Mapping", "An argument map is a visual diagram showing how premises support conclusions, including sub-arguments, objections, and rebuttals. Mapping forces hidden structure into the open and research suggests it measurably improves reasoning skills. This lesson teaches you to draw simple box-and-arrow maps of real arguments.", ["Paper or whiteboard", "Pen or markers"], ["Write the main conclusion in a box at the top of the page", "Place each premise in its own box below, with arrows pointing to the conclusion it supports", "Distinguish independent premises (each supports the conclusion alone) from linked premises (which only work together)", "Add any objections in boxes with arrows attacking the premise or inference they challenge", "Review the map and ask which single box, if removed, would collapse the argument"], "What is the benefit of drawing an argument as a map instead of leaving it as prose?", "Mapping makes the structure explicit, showing exactly which premises support which conclusions and where objections strike, making gaps and weak links much easier to spot", "Start by mapping short letters to the editor or social media arguments; they usually have one conclusion and two or three premises, perfect for practice."),
    ("Finding Hidden Assumptions", "Most real arguments rest on unstated premises the arguer takes for granted, such as 'this politician was a businessman, so he'll manage the economy well', which silently assumes business skill transfers to governing. Surfacing these hidden assumptions is often the fastest way to evaluate an argument, because the weakest premise is frequently the one never stated. This lesson teaches systematic techniques for uncovering them.", ["Notebook", "Pen"], ["Write out an argument's stated premises and conclusion in standard form", "Ask what else must be true for the premises to actually support the conclusion", "State each hidden assumption explicitly as an added premise", "Evaluate whether each surfaced assumption is plausible, questionable, or clearly false", "Practice on advertisements, which are dense with unstated assumptions about what you want and need"], "Why are hidden assumptions often the weakest part of an argument?", "Because they were never stated, they were never examined or defended, so they can smuggle in questionable claims that would be challenged if made explicit", "Advertisements are ideal practice material: 'Athletes drink Brand X' hides the assumption that what athletes drink is good for you."),
    ("Evaluating Analogies", "Arguments by analogy claim that because two things are alike in some respects, they are alike in another respect, such as comparing a national budget to a household budget. An analogy's strength depends on whether the similarities are relevant to the conclusion and whether important disanalogies undermine it. This lesson teaches you to test analogies rather than being swayed by vivid ones.", ["Notebook", "Pen"], ["Identify the two things being compared and the conclusion the analogy is meant to support", "List the stated and implied similarities between them", "Ask whether those similarities are actually relevant to the conclusion being drawn", "List relevant differences (disanalogies) and judge whether they break the comparison", "Practice on the household-budget analogy for government spending, noting relevant differences like currency issuance and borrowing capacity"], "What determines whether an argument by analogy is strong or weak?", "Whether the similarities between the two cases are relevant to the conclusion, and whether there are relevant differences that undermine the comparison", "A vivid analogy is not automatically a good one; the more emotionally compelling a comparison is, the more carefully you should check its relevance."),
    ("The Power of Counterexamples", "A counterexample is a single case that shows a general claim to be false, making it one of the most efficient tools in reasoning: 'all swans are white' dies the moment one black swan appears. Searching for counterexamples to your own claims is also a core habit of rigorous thinkers. This lesson teaches how to construct and deploy counterexamples fairly.", ["Notebook", "Pen"], ["Take a universal claim such as 'lying is always wrong' or 'all successful people wake up early'", "Deliberately search for a single real or hypothetical case where the claim fails", "Check that your counterexample genuinely fits the claim's terms rather than attacking a different claim", "Revise the original claim to survive the counterexample, for example from 'always' to 'usually', and note how much weaker it becomes", "Apply the same test to one of your own confident generalizations"], "How many counterexamples are needed to refute a claim of the form 'all X are Y'?", "Just one; a single genuine case of an X that is not Y makes the universal claim false", "Before stating a claim with 'always', 'never', or 'everyone', spend ten seconds hunting for a counterexample yourself; you'll often soften the claim and be more accurate."),
    ("The Principle of Charity and Steelmanning", "The principle of charity says you should interpret others' arguments in their strongest, most reasonable form, and steelmanning goes further by actively constructing the best possible version of an opposing view before responding. This is the opposite of the straw man fallacy and it makes your own rebuttals genuinely persuasive rather than hollow. This lesson teaches both habits.", ["Notebook", "Pen"], ["Choose a viewpoint you disagree with on a real issue", "Write the strongest, most reasonable version of that position, using its best arguments and evidence", "Check your version with the test: would a thoughtful holder of that view accept your summary as fair?", "Only after steelmanning, write your response, addressing the strong version rather than a weak caricature", "Notice how your own position sharpened or shifted during the exercise"], "What is the difference between steelmanning and straw manning an argument?", "Steelmanning means engaging with the strongest possible version of an opposing argument, while straw manning means misrepresenting it as weaker than it is in order to knock it down easily", "In a disagreement, try saying 'So your strongest point is...' and stating it fairly before responding; it lowers the temperature and improves the discussion instantly."),

    # ---------------- Logical fallacies ----------------
    ("Ad Hominem and Tu Quoque", "The ad hominem fallacy attacks the person making an argument rather than the argument itself, and tu quoque ('you too') is a variant that deflects criticism by accusing the critic of hypocrisy. Both are fallacies because a claim's truth is independent of who states it, and a hypocrite can still be right. This lesson teaches you to spot and answer both moves.", ["Notebook", "Pen"], ["Study the pattern: 'You can't trust her argument on tax policy; she's been divorced twice' attacks the person, not the argument", "Study tu quoque: 'You say smoking is unhealthy, but you smoke!' does not make smoking healthy", "Learn the exception: attacking credibility is relevant when someone's testimony (not their argument) is the evidence", "Find two real examples in comment sections or political coverage and identify whether the attack targets the argument or the person", "Practice responding: 'That's about me, not about whether the claim is true'"], "Why doesn't pointing out a person's hypocrisy refute their argument?", "Because an argument's soundness depends on its premises and logic, not on the behavior of the person making it; a hypocrite can still state a true claim with valid reasoning", "Personal attacks are relevant only when someone's personal testimony is itself the evidence; otherwise redirect to the actual claim."),
    ("The Straw Man Fallacy", "A straw man fallacy misrepresents someone's position as a weaker or more extreme version and then attacks that distortion instead of the real view. It is among the most common fallacies in political debate and online argument, and it works because the distorted version is genuinely easy to defeat. This lesson teaches you to detect straw men aimed at others and at yourself.", ["Notebook", "Pen"], ["Study the pattern: 'She wants better funding for schools' becomes 'She thinks throwing money at problems solves everything'", "Compare the original claim and the attacked claim side by side and list what was added, removed, or exaggerated", "Find one real example in a debate, article, or comment thread", "Practice the countermove: restate your actual position calmly and ask your critic to address that version", "Check your own arguments for accidental straw manning by asking whether your opponent would accept your summary of their view"], "What is the straw man fallacy?", "Misrepresenting someone's argument as a weaker or more extreme version and then refuting the distortion instead of the actual position", "When your view is straw manned, don't defend the distortion; calmly restate what you actually said and ask them to respond to that."),
    ("False Dilemma and Slippery Slope", "A false dilemma presents only two options when more exist ('you're either with us or against us'), while a slippery slope claims one step will inevitably lead to an extreme outcome without justifying the chain of events. Both fallacies distort a decision space: one shrinks it artificially, the other extends it without evidence. This lesson teaches you to spot and dismantle both.", ["Notebook", "Pen"], ["Study false dilemmas: 'Either we cut the budget or we go bankrupt' hides options like raising revenue or partial cuts", "Practice generating a third option for any either/or framing you encounter", "Study slippery slopes: 'If we allow X, next it will be Y, then Z' and note that each link in the chain needs its own evidence", "Distinguish fallacious slopes from legitimate ones where a causal mechanism for each step is actually demonstrated", "Find one real example of each fallacy in advertising, politics, or everyday conversation"], "What makes a slippery slope argument fallacious rather than legitimate?", "It is fallacious when the chain of consequences is asserted without evidence for each link; it can be legitimate if each step's causal connection is actually supported", "Whenever you hear 'either/or', pause and try to name a third option; its existence instantly exposes a false dilemma."),
    ("Appeal to Authority and Appeal to Emotion", "An appeal to authority is fallacious when it cites someone without relevant expertise, misrepresents expert consensus, or treats authority as settling a question evidence should settle; citing genuine, relevant expert consensus is reasonable. An appeal to emotion substitutes feelings like fear, pity, or outrage for evidence. This lesson teaches you to distinguish legitimate from illegitimate uses of both.", ["Notebook", "Pen"], ["Learn when citing authority is legitimate: the authority has relevant expertise and reflects the consensus in their actual field", "Study the fallacy: a famous physicist endorsing a nutrition supplement carries no special weight on nutrition", "Study appeals to emotion: an ad showing sad animals may motivate donating without saying anything about a charity's effectiveness", "Note that emotions can flag issues worth caring about; the fallacy is letting emotion replace evidence about facts", "Collect one advertisement that uses a misplaced authority and one that runs on pure emotion, and write what evidence is actually missing"], "When is citing an expert legitimate rather than a fallacious appeal to authority?", "When the expert has genuine expertise in the specific relevant field and their view reflects the consensus of that field, rather than being a celebrity or out-of-field endorsement", "Ask two questions of any cited expert: 'Expert in what, exactly?' and 'Do most experts in that field agree?'"),
    ("Red Herring and Circular Reasoning", "A red herring diverts attention from the issue at hand by introducing an irrelevant topic, while circular reasoning (begging the question) assumes the conclusion within the premises, as in 'the Bible is true because the Bible says so'. Both can sound persuasive in fast conversation because they mimic the rhythm of real argument. This lesson teaches you to catch each in real time.", ["Notebook", "Pen"], ["Study red herrings: asked about missed deadlines, a manager answers by praising the team's dedication, changing the subject", "Practice the countermove: 'That may be true, but the question was...' and restate the original issue", "Study circular reasoning: 'He's trustworthy because he never lies' restates the conclusion as its own support", "Test arguments for circularity by asking whether accepting the premise already requires accepting the conclusion", "Find one example of each in an interview, debate, or discussion thread"], "What does it mean for an argument to 'beg the question'?", "It assumes the truth of the conclusion within its own premises, so the argument provides no independent support; the reasoning runs in a circle", "In interviews and debates, track the original question on paper; red herrings become obvious when you can see the question that never got answered."),
    ("Hasty Generalization and Post Hoc Reasoning", "A hasty generalization draws a broad conclusion from too small or unrepresentative a sample ('my grandfather smoked and lived to 95, so smoking isn't harmful'), while post hoc ergo propter hoc ('after this, therefore because of this') assumes that because B followed A, A caused B. These two fallacies underlie a huge share of everyday superstition, health misinformation, and stereotyping. This lesson teaches how to catch both.", ["Notebook", "Pen"], ["Study hasty generalization: judging a whole city by one visit, or a group by one member", "Ask of any generalization: how large and how representative was the sample behind it?", "Study post hoc: 'I wore my lucky socks and we won' or crediting a remedy for a cold that would have ended anyway", "Learn alternative explanations for sequences: coincidence, regression to normal, or a third factor causing both", "Write down one generalization and one causal claim you personally believe, and stress-test each with these questions"], "What does 'post hoc ergo propter hoc' mean, and why is it a fallacy?", "It is Latin for 'after this, therefore because of this'; it is fallacious because mere temporal sequence does not establish causation, since coincidence or other factors may explain the outcome", "When a remedy 'works', ask what would have happened anyway; many illnesses improve on their own, which is exactly when people try remedies."),
    ("No True Scotsman and Equivocation", "The no true Scotsman fallacy rescues a generalization from counterexamples by redefining the group ('no true Scotsman would do that'), making the claim unfalsifiable. Equivocation exploits a word's multiple meanings mid-argument, as when 'law' shifts between legal statutes and laws of nature. Both fallacies corrupt arguments through slippery definitions. This lesson teaches you to pin definitions down.", ["Notebook", "Pen"], ["Study no true Scotsman: 'No real fan would criticize the team' redefines 'real fan' to dodge every counterexample", "Spot the tell: group membership gets redefined precisely when a counterexample appears", "Study equivocation: 'Theory' means rigorous explanatory framework in science but guess in casual speech; sliding between them misleads", "Practice the fix: ask 'Can we define that term and keep the definition fixed?' before continuing the argument", "Find one example of each fallacy in sports, politics, or religion discussions"], "How does the no true Scotsman fallacy make a claim unfalsifiable?", "By redefining the group to exclude any counterexample, so no possible evidence could ever count against the generalization", "Arguments that hinge on one word (freedom, natural, theory, real) usually need that word defined first; insist on a fixed definition before proceeding."),
    ("Sunk Cost and Gambler's Fallacies", "The sunk cost fallacy is continuing an endeavor because of resources already invested rather than future prospects, like finishing a bad movie because you paid for the ticket. The gambler's fallacy is believing past independent random events change future probabilities, like expecting red on a roulette wheel after five blacks. Both fallacies distort decisions by dragging irrelevant history into forward-looking choices. This lesson covers recognizing and resisting each.", ["Notebook", "Pen", "Coin (for the probability demonstration)"], ["Study sunk cost: money and time already spent cannot be recovered, so only future costs and benefits should drive the decision", "Practice the reframe: 'If I were starting fresh today with no history, would I choose this?'", "Study the gambler's fallacy by flipping a coin: after three heads, the next flip is still exactly 50/50 because flips are independent", "Distinguish independent events (coin flips, roulette) from dependent ones (cards drawn without replacement), where past outcomes do matter", "Identify one commitment in your own life that sunk cost may be sustaining, and evaluate it on future value alone"], "After a fair coin lands heads five times in a row, what is the probability of heads on the next flip?", "Exactly 50%, because each flip is independent; believing tails is 'due' is the gambler's fallacy", "For any ongoing commitment, ask the fresh-start question: 'Knowing what I know now, would I start this today?' If not, the rest is sunk cost."),

    # ---------------- Cognitive biases ----------------
    ("Confirmation Bias", "Confirmation bias is the tendency to search for, interpret, and remember information that confirms what we already believe while ignoring or discounting contradicting evidence. It is arguably the most consequential cognitive bias, shaping everything from news consumption to scientific error to relationship conflicts. This lesson covers how it works and concrete counter-strategies.", ["Notebook", "Pen"], ["Notice the mechanism: we ask 'Can I believe this?' about agreeable claims but 'Must I believe this?' about disagreeable ones", "Pick a belief you hold and deliberately search for the strongest evidence and arguments against it", "Practice 'consider the opposite': before settling a question, spend two minutes arguing the other side in writing", "Diversify inputs: identify one thoughtful source you disagree with and read it regularly", "Track predictions you make and check them against outcomes, since memory alone will flatter your track record"], "What is confirmation bias?", "The tendency to seek out, interpret, and remember information that supports existing beliefs while ignoring or downplaying evidence that contradicts them", "You cannot simply switch confirmation bias off; the workable defense is procedural, like deliberately assigning yourself the search for disconfirming evidence."),
    ("Anchoring and Framing Effects", "Anchoring is the tendency for the first number or piece of information encountered to disproportionately influence subsequent judgments, which is why a '$100, now $60' tag feels like a bargain. Framing effects mean the same facts presented differently ('90% survival rate' vs '10% mortality rate') lead to different decisions. Both biases were documented extensively by psychologists Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman. This lesson teaches you to notice and neutralize them.", ["Notebook", "Pen"], ["Study anchoring: initial listed prices, first offers in negotiation, and even irrelevant numbers pull estimates toward them", "Counter anchors by generating your own independent estimate before looking at any suggested number", "Study framing: rewrite '90% fat free' as '10% fat' and notice the shift in how it feels", "Practice reframing any statistic you encounter into its complementary form before reacting", "Spot three anchors or frames in one shopping trip or news session and write down the neutral restatement"], "Why does a crossed-out higher price make a sale price feel like a better deal?", "The original price acts as an anchor that pulls your sense of the item's value upward, making the sale price seem low by comparison regardless of the item's actual worth", "Before any negotiation or big purchase, write down your own valuation first; an anchor works best on a mind that arrives empty."),
    ("Availability Heuristic and Negativity Bias", "The availability heuristic leads us to judge how likely something is by how easily examples come to mind, which is why heavily reported dangers like plane crashes feel more common than statistically larger risks like car accidents. Negativity bias means negative events grip our attention and memory more strongly than equally significant positive ones. Together they systematically distort our picture of how dangerous and bad the world is. This lesson covers both and their fixes.", ["Notebook", "Pen"], ["Study availability: vivid, recent, and emotional events are easy to recall, so we overestimate their frequency", "Test yourself: guess whether more people die annually from sharks or from falling out of bed, then look up the actual figures", "Study negativity bias: notice how a single criticism outweighs several compliments in your memory of a day", "Counter both by asking 'What do the base rates and statistics say?' instead of 'What examples come to mind?'", "Audit your media diet for a day, noting how many stories are rare-but-vivid events versus common-but-boring risks"], "What is the availability heuristic?", "Judging the probability or frequency of events by how easily examples come to mind, which overweights vivid, recent, and heavily reported events", "When something feels common or dangerous, ask whether you're remembering statistics or headlines; the news reports events precisely because they are rare."),
    ("The Dunning-Kruger Effect and Hindsight Bias", "The Dunning-Kruger effect, documented by psychologists David Dunning and Justin Kruger, is the tendency for people with low skill in a domain to overestimate their competence because they lack the knowledge needed to recognize their own errors. Hindsight bias is the 'I knew it all along' tendency to see past events as having been predictable after they happen. Both biases inflate our confidence in our own judgment. This lesson covers recognizing them in yourself.", ["Notebook", "Pen"], ["Study Dunning-Kruger: assessing skill in a domain requires the very expertise beginners lack, so early confidence often exceeds early competence", "Recall a skill you now know well and how much you underestimated its depth as a beginner", "Study hindsight bias: after outcomes are known, we misremember our predictions as more accurate than they were", "Counter hindsight bias by writing predictions down before outcomes occur, with your confidence level attached", "Before your next confident claim in an unfamiliar domain, honestly rate your actual experience in it"], "Why do beginners in a field often overestimate their own competence?", "Because accurately judging skill requires the same knowledge as the skill itself, beginners lack the expertise to recognize their own mistakes and knowledge gaps", "Keep a written prediction journal; nothing punctures 'I knew it all along' like your own dated, written forecast saying otherwise."),
    ("Survivorship Bias", "Survivorship bias is the error of drawing conclusions only from the people or things that survived a selection process while overlooking the failures that were filtered out and became invisible. The classic example comes from WWII, when statistician Abraham Wald realized returning bombers should be armored where they showed no bullet holes, because planes hit in those spots never made it home. This lesson teaches you to always ask about the missing data.", ["Notebook", "Pen"], ["Study the Wald bomber example: the visible damage on survivors marks the survivable hits, not the fatal ones", "Apply it to success advice: for every dropout billionaire there are thousands of dropouts who never got rich and are never interviewed", "Practice the key question: 'Who or what is missing from this sample, and why?'", "Analyze a claim like 'old buildings were built better' by noting that poorly built old buildings already collapsed", "Find one modern example in business advice, fitness testimonials, or investment track records"], "In the WWII bomber example, why did Wald recommend armoring the areas without bullet holes?", "Because the data came only from planes that returned; planes hit in the unmarked areas were shot down and never counted, so those areas were the fatal vulnerabilities", "Whenever you study winners for their secrets, force yourself to ask how many losers did exactly the same things."),
    ("In-Group Bias and the Fundamental Attribution Error", "In-group bias is the tendency to favor, trust, and excuse members of our own group while judging outsiders more harshly. The fundamental attribution error is explaining others' behavior by their character ('he's rude') while explaining our own by circumstances ('I was having a bad day'). Together these biases fuel prejudice, team conflicts, and polarized politics. This lesson covers spotting both in daily judgments.", ["Notebook", "Pen"], ["Study in-group bias: the same argument feels stronger from an ally and weaker from an opponent; test this on yourself with a swap exercise", "Practice the source swap: imagine a claim you just accepted came from the opposing side, and check whether your evaluation changes", "Study the fundamental attribution error: when someone cuts you off in traffic, notice the leap to 'jerk' versus your own 'I was late'", "Counter it by generating one plausible situational explanation for the next annoying behavior you witness", "Review a recent conflict and rewrite it attributing your behavior to character and theirs to circumstances, reversing your default"], "What is the fundamental attribution error?", "The tendency to explain other people's behavior by their character or disposition while explaining our own behavior by situational factors", "Judge arguments with the source hidden when you can; if knowing who said it would change your verdict, bias is doing some of your thinking."),

    # ---------------- Evidence and research literacy ----------------
    ("Evaluating Sources: Credibility Checks", "Not all sources deserve equal trust, and evaluating credibility means checking who is behind the information, what their expertise and incentives are, when it was published, and whether other reliable sources corroborate it. A systematic checklist beats gut feelings, which are easily fooled by professional design and confident tone. This lesson provides a practical source-evaluation routine.", ["Computer or phone with internet access", "Notebook"], ["Identify the author and publisher, and check their relevant expertise and track record", "Check the date, since accurate-when-written information can be outdated now", "Look for incentives: who funds the source and what do they gain from you believing it?", "Check whether claims cite evidence you can follow to its origin, or just assert", "Corroborate: see whether independent, reliable sources report the same core facts"], "Name three things to check when evaluating whether a source is credible.", "Any three of: the author's relevant expertise, the publisher's track record, the publication date, funding and incentives, whether claims cite verifiable evidence, and corroboration by independent sources", "A polished website and confident tone signal nothing about accuracy; design is cheap while expertise and evidence are not."),
    ("Correlation vs. Causation", "Two things occurring together (correlation) does not establish that one causes the other: ice cream sales and drowning deaths rise together because both are caused by summer weather, a classic third-variable effect. Reverse causation and coincidence are other reasons correlations mislead, and untangling them is the reason careful experiments exist. This lesson teaches the standard alternative explanations to check before accepting any causal claim.", ["Notebook", "Pen"], ["Learn the four possibilities behind 'A correlates with B': A causes B, B causes A, a third factor C causes both, or coincidence", "Analyze the ice cream and drowning correlation and identify the third variable (warm weather)", "Practice on a headline like 'People who drink wine live longer' by generating third-variable and reverse-causation explanations", "Learn why randomized controlled experiments support causal claims: random assignment breaks the link to third variables", "Collect one news headline this week that states or implies causation from a mere correlation"], "If ice cream sales and drowning deaths rise and fall together, does ice cream cause drowning?", "No; both are driven by a third variable, warm summer weather, which increases both swimming and ice cream consumption", "For any 'X linked to Y' headline, run the four-explanation checklist: X causes Y, Y causes X, something causes both, or chance."),
    ("Sample Size and Representativeness", "A study's conclusions are only as good as its sample: small samples produce noisy, unreliable results, and unrepresentative samples produce systematically biased ones no matter how large they are. A famous cautionary tale is the 1936 Literary Digest poll, which surveyed over two million people yet wrongly predicted the US presidential election because its sample skewed wealthy. This lesson teaches what to check about any study's sample.", ["Notebook", "Pen"], ["Understand sample size: results from 10 people swing wildly by chance, while results from 10,000 are far more stable", "Understand representativeness: a sample must resemble the population the conclusion is about", "Study the Literary Digest case: a huge but skewed sample (drawn largely from telephone and car owners during the Depression) still failed", "Learn about self-selection bias: people who volunteer opinions differ systematically from people who don't", "For the next survey or study you see, find the sample size and ask who was included, who was left out, and who chose to respond"], "Can a very large sample still produce misleading results?", "Yes; if the sample is unrepresentative of the target population, as in the 1936 Literary Digest poll, size does not fix the systematic bias", "The first two questions for any statistic: 'How many people?' and 'Which people?'; a headline that hides both is hiding the weakness."),
    ("Base Rates: The Forgotten Denominator", "The base rate is how common something is in the general population, and ignoring it, known as base rate neglect, produces wildly wrong conclusions from accurate data. A medical test that is 99% accurate for a disease affecting 1 in 10,000 people still produces mostly false positives, because the huge number of healthy people tested generates more false alarms than there are actual patients. This lesson teaches you to always ask for the denominator.", ["Notebook", "Pen", "Calculator"], ["Learn what a base rate is: the background frequency of a condition or event in the relevant population", "Work the classic example: test 10,000 people for a 1-in-10,000 disease with a 1% false-positive rate, and count roughly 100 false positives against 1 true case", "Conclude that a positive result here means roughly a 1% chance of disease, not 99%", "Apply base rates elsewhere: judging professions, risks, and rare events all require the background frequency", "Practice asking 'Out of how many?' for every scary statistic you encounter this week"], "Why can a positive result from a 99%-accurate test for a rare disease still probably be a false alarm?", "Because when the disease is very rare, the many false positives from the huge healthy population outnumber the few true positives, so most positive results come from healthy people", "Whenever a claim involves rare events, sketch a population of 10,000 people and count the groups; the arithmetic routinely overturns intuition."),
    ("Percentages, Relative Risk, and Absolute Risk", "A headline like 'this food doubles your cancer risk' can describe a jump from 1 in 100,000 to 2 in 100,000, which is why the difference between relative risk (the ratio between groups) and absolute risk (the actual probability change) matters enormously. Reporting relative risk without the baseline is one of the most common ways accurate numbers create false impressions. This lesson teaches you to convert alarming percentages into real-world frequencies.", ["Notebook", "Pen", "Calculator"], ["Learn the definitions: absolute risk is your actual probability, relative risk compares one group's risk to another's", "Work an example: a '50% increased risk' on a 2-in-1,000 baseline means 3 in 1,000, an absolute increase of 1 person per 1,000", "Translate percentages into natural frequencies, such as 'X out of 1,000 people', which are much harder to misread", "Watch for the reverse trick: benefits stated in relative terms ('cuts risk in half!') on tiny baselines", "Find one health or finance headline using relative risk and compute the absolute change it actually describes"], "A headline says a food 'doubles' a disease risk that affects 1 in 100,000 people. What is the new absolute risk?", "2 in 100,000, an absolute increase of just one case per 100,000 people, despite the alarming-sounding relative doubling", "Always ask 'From what, to what?' when you hear a percentage change; refuse to be alarmed or impressed until you know the baseline."),
    ("Reading a Scientific Study Critically", "A single scientific paper is a data point, not a verdict, and reading one critically means checking the study design, sample, controls, effect size, and limitations rather than just the abstract's conclusion. Key distinctions include observational studies versus randomized controlled trials, and statistical significance versus practical importance. This lesson gives you a practical checklist for reading research without a science degree.", ["A published study or its abstract (many are free on PubMed or Google Scholar)", "Notebook"], ["Identify the study design: randomized controlled trial, cohort study, case-control study, or cross-sectional survey, in roughly descending order of causal strength", "Check the sample: how many subjects, how selected, and were they humans or, as in many headlines, mice?", "Look for a control group and whether subjects and researchers were blinded", "Distinguish statistical significance (unlikely due to chance) from effect size (large enough to matter in practice)", "Read the authors' own limitations section, which usually states the caveats headlines omit"], "What is the difference between statistical significance and practical significance in a study?", "Statistical significance means a result is unlikely to be due to chance alone, while practical significance concerns whether the effect is large enough to matter in the real world; a tiny, unimportant effect can be statistically significant in a big study", "Before trusting a headline about a study, find the answer to one question: was it in humans, and how many? A shocking number of headlines trace back to small mouse studies."),
    ("Peer Review and Meta-Analyses: How Science Vets Itself", "Peer review is the process by which journals send submitted research to independent experts who check its methods and reasoning before publication; it filters out obvious errors but does not verify raw data or reliably catch fraud, so peer-reviewed findings can still be wrong. Above any single study sits the meta-analysis, which statistically combines many studies on the same question, and the systematic review, which comprehensively surveys them, making them the strongest tier of the evidence hierarchy. This lesson teaches what each layer guarantees and how to seek evidence syntheses instead of cherry-picked single studies.", ["Computer with internet access", "Notebook"], ["Learn what peer review checks: methodology, reasoning, and novelty as reported by the authors; reviewers rarely see raw data or rerun experiments", "Learn the evidence hierarchy: meta-analyses and systematic reviews, then randomized trials, then observational studies, then case reports and anecdotes", "Understand why single studies conflict: small samples, chance, differing methods, and publication bias favoring exciting positive results", "Practice searching for a systematic review (for example via the Cochrane Library) on a health question you care about", "When someone cites 'a study', ask what the totality of studies on that question shows"], "Why is a meta-analysis generally stronger evidence than a single peer-reviewed study?", "It combines data across many studies, averaging out chance results and individual study quirks, whereas even a peer-reviewed single study can be wrong since review checks methods and reasoning but does not verify data or replicate results", "Treat 'peer-reviewed' as 'passed a meaningful screening', not 'proven true'; when a question matters, look for a systematic review or meta-analysis rather than any single study."),
    ("Spotting Misleading Graphs and Statistics", "Charts can technically show true numbers while creating false impressions through truncated y-axes that exaggerate small differences, cherry-picked date ranges, dual axes, and misleading area scaling. Statistical language can mislead the same way, as when a shifting definition or a changed denominator quietly reshapes a trend. This lesson trains your eye to audit charts before trusting them.", ["Examples of charts from news or social media", "Notebook", "Pen"], ["Check the y-axis first: does it start at zero, and if not, is the zoom exaggerating a small difference?", "Check the x-axis: is the date range cherry-picked to start at a convenient low or high point?", "Check definitions and denominators: totals versus per-capita rates can tell opposite stories about the same data", "Watch for dual y-axes and 3D or area effects, which invite false comparisons", "Redraw one misleading chart you find with a zero baseline and a fuller date range, and compare impressions"], "How can a truncated y-axis make a chart misleading even when the data is accurate?", "By starting the axis near the data values instead of zero, small differences are visually magnified, making a trivial change look dramatic", "The two-second chart audit: look at where the y-axis starts and where the x-axis begins before you look at the line."),
    ("The Replication Crisis", "The replication crisis refers to the discovery, prominent since the early 2010s, that many published findings in psychology, medicine, and other fields fail to reproduce when independent researchers rerun the studies; a landmark 2015 effort by the Open Science Collaboration successfully replicated only about 36-39% of 100 sampled psychology studies. Causes include publication bias, small samples, and questionable research practices like p-hacking. This lesson explains the crisis and the reforms addressing it, and what it means for how you consume science news.", ["Notebook", "Pen"], ["Learn what replication means: independent researchers repeating a study to see whether the finding holds", "Understand the causes: pressure to publish novel positive results, small samples, flexible analysis choices (p-hacking), and journals' bias against null results", "Learn famous casualties: once-celebrated effects such as ego depletion and social priming findings have repeatedly failed to replicate", "Learn the reforms: preregistration of hypotheses, larger samples, open data, and registered reports", "Adjust your habits: give more weight to replicated, preregistered findings and less to single splashy studies"], "What is the replication crisis?", "The finding that a large share of published scientific results, notably in psychology and medicine, fail to reproduce when independently retested, due to factors like publication bias, small samples, and p-hacking", "A finding's age and fame are no guarantee it replicated; when a result matters to you, search its name plus the word 'replication' before relying on it."),

    # ---------------- Media and information literacy ----------------
    ("Misinformation vs. Disinformation", "Misinformation is false or misleading information spread without intent to deceive, while disinformation is false information created or spread deliberately to mislead, often for political or financial gain; malinformation, a related term, is genuine information deployed maliciously, such as leaked private data. Understanding intent and mechanics helps you respond appropriately, since a mistaken relative and a troll farm call for different reactions. This lesson maps the misleading-information landscape.", ["Notebook", "Pen"], ["Learn the definitions: misinformation (false, spread innocently), disinformation (false, spread deliberately), malinformation (true but weaponized)", "Study how each spreads: sincere sharing by alarmed people versus coordinated campaigns and fake accounts", "Learn common disinformation techniques: fabricated quotes, miscaptioned real images, imposter news sites, and manufactured outrage", "Practice the pause: strong emotion (outrage, vindication) upon reading something is a signal to verify before sharing", "Trace one viral claim you've seen back to its original source and classify what kind of misleading information it was, if any"], "What is the difference between misinformation and disinformation?", "Misinformation is false information shared without deceptive intent, while disinformation is false information deliberately created or spread to deceive", "The stronger your emotional reaction to a post, the more valuable it is to someone that you share it without checking; treat outrage as a verification trigger."),
    ("Lateral Reading: How Fact-Checkers Verify", "Lateral reading is the verification technique used by professional fact-checkers: instead of studying a website itself (vertical reading), you open new tabs and search for what other independent sources say about the site and its claims. Research by the Stanford History Education Group found fact-checkers using this method vastly outperformed even PhD historians who stayed on the original page. This lesson teaches you the technique step by step.", ["Computer or phone with internet access", "Notebook"], ["When you land on an unfamiliar site, resist evaluating it by its own About page, design, or domain name", "Open new tabs and search the organization's name plus words like 'funding', 'credibility', or 'bias'", "Search the specific claim to see whether established fact-checking outlets or reputable news sources have covered it", "Find the claim's original source; viral claims often distort a real study, quote, or event", "Practice by lateral-reading one unfamiliar source this week and timing yourself; it typically takes under two minutes"], "How does lateral reading differ from evaluating a website by reading it closely?", "Lateral reading means leaving the site to check what independent sources say about it and its claims, rather than trusting the site's own presentation, which is under its control", "The About page is the site talking about itself; the first rule of verification is that self-descriptions don't count as evidence."),
    ("Deepfakes and Synthetic Media", "Deepfakes are AI-generated or AI-manipulated audio, images, and video that can convincingly depict real people saying or doing things they never did, and generative tools have made such synthetic media cheap and abundant. Visual artifacts alone are increasingly unreliable for detection, so verification is shifting to provenance: where a file came from and whether independent sources corroborate the event. This lesson covers realistic detection and verification habits.", ["Computer or phone with internet access", "Notebook"], ["Understand what synthetic media can now fake: faces, voices, and whole scenes, well enough to fool casual viewing", "Check imperfect visual cues: distorted hands and text, inconsistent lighting and shadows, unnatural blinking or lip-sync; treat these as hints, never proof", "Prioritize provenance: who first posted this, when, and is there an original, higher-quality version?", "Use reverse image search to find earlier versions and check whether real events are corroborated by multiple independent outlets", "Adopt the standing rule: the more consequential a shocking clip is, the more independent confirmation it needs before you believe or share it"], "Why are visual artifacts alone unreliable for detecting deepfakes?", "Because generation tools improve constantly, the telltale glitches keep disappearing; verification therefore depends more on provenance and independent corroboration than on spotting visual flaws", "For any explosive clip, ask 'Who else is reporting this?'; a genuinely real major event is almost never carried by only one anonymous account."),
    ("Echo Chambers and Filter Bubbles", "An echo chamber is a social environment where you mainly encounter beliefs that match your own and dissent is absent or discredited, while a filter bubble is the related effect of personalization algorithms feeding you content similar to what you previously engaged with. Both narrow the evidence reaching you and can make fringe views feel like consensus. This lesson covers recognizing these dynamics and deliberately engineering exposure to diverse views.", ["Computer or phone with internet access", "Notebook"], ["Understand the mechanism: recommendation algorithms optimize for engagement, and agreeable or outrage-inducing content engages", "Audit your information diet: list your main news and social sources and honestly note how many challenge your views", "Notice echo chamber warning signs: opponents seem cartoonishly evil or stupid, and you can't state their actual arguments", "Deliberately add one or two substantive sources from different perspectives, chosen for quality rather than extremity", "Test yourself monthly: can you pass an ideological Turing test, stating the other side's view so they'd accept it as fair?"], "What is the difference between an echo chamber and a filter bubble?", "An echo chamber is a social environment where dissenting views are absent or actively discredited, while a filter bubble results from algorithms personalizing your feed toward content you already engage with; both narrow your exposure", "If you cannot state the other side's strongest argument in a way they would endorse, you don't yet know their position; you know your side's version of it."),
    ("Evaluating Expert Claims and Consensus", "Because no one can verify everything personally, rational belief often depends on trusting the right experts, which requires distinguishing relevant expertise, individual opinion, and field-wide consensus. A credentialed contrarian is newsworthy precisely because they diverge from what most qualified experts have concluded, and treating lone dissent as equal to consensus is a common media distortion called false balance. This lesson teaches practical rules for deciding when expert claims deserve your trust.", ["Notebook", "Pen", "Computer with internet access"], ["Check relevance: is this person an expert in the specific field of the claim, not just credentialed in something?", "Distinguish one expert's opinion from field consensus, which aggregates many independent expert judgments and is far more reliable", "Investigate incentives: is the expert selling something, funded by an interested party, or building a contrarian brand?", "Recognize false balance: presenting one dissenting scientist opposite the consensus as a 50/50 debate misrepresents the evidence", "For a live topic, look up what major scientific bodies and systematic reviews conclude, rather than which individual is loudest"], "Why is scientific consensus more reliable than the view of a single credentialed expert?", "Consensus aggregates the independent judgments of many qualified experts evaluating the total evidence, so individual errors and biases tend to cancel out, whereas any single expert can be mistaken, biased, or incentivized", "When a headline says 'expert claims...', check two things: expert in what, and what does the rest of the field say?"),

    # ---------------- Applied decision-making ----------------
    ("Bayesian Thinking Basics", "Bayesian thinking, named after the 18th-century mathematician Thomas Bayes, treats beliefs as probabilities that should be updated incrementally as new evidence arrives, starting from a prior based on base rates. The core question is how much more likely the evidence would be if your hypothesis were true than if it were false, and updating by that amount rather than lurching between certainty and denial. This lesson teaches the informal habit without heavy math.", ["Notebook", "Pen"], ["Start with a prior: before new evidence, how likely was the claim, given base rates?", "When evidence arrives, ask the key question: how much more expected is this evidence if the claim is true than if it's false?", "Update proportionally: strong, surprising evidence moves you a lot; weak, easily-explained evidence moves you a little", "Practice with an everyday case: a friend is late (weak evidence of a problem, since traffic is common) versus a friend missing all day (strong evidence)", "Express updated beliefs as rough percentages rather than flipping between 'definitely' and 'no way'"], "In Bayesian terms, what makes a piece of evidence strong support for a hypothesis?", "The evidence is much more likely to occur if the hypothesis is true than if it is false; evidence equally expected either way provides little or no support", "Ask of any evidence: 'Would I expect to see this even if I were wrong?' If yes, it barely moves the needle."),
    ("Expected Value Reasoning", "Expected value is the average outcome of a decision if it were repeated many times, computed by multiplying each outcome's value by its probability and summing the results. It explains why a 10% chance at $100 (worth $10 on average) beats a sure $5, why lottery tickets are systematically losing purchases, and why insurance can be rational despite negative expected monetary value once the catastrophic downside is weighed. This lesson teaches the calculation and its limits.", ["Notebook", "Pen", "Calculator"], ["Learn the formula: expected value equals the sum of each outcome's probability times its value", "Work an example: a coin flip paying $10 for heads and costing $4 for tails has EV of (0.5 x 10) + (0.5 x -4) = $3, a good bet", "Compute a lottery-style example and see how a huge prize times a tiny probability yields less than the ticket price", "Learn the limits: for rare, unrepeatable, or ruinous outcomes, consider risk tolerance and worst cases, not just the average", "Apply EV to a real choice you face, estimating probabilities honestly and writing out the calculation"], "How do you calculate the expected value of a decision?", "Multiply the value of each possible outcome by its probability and add the results, giving the average result the decision would produce over many repetitions", "Expected value thinking shines on repeated small decisions; for rare decisions that could ruin you, weigh the worst case, not just the average."),
    ("Decision Matrices", "A decision matrix (also called a weighted scoring model or Pugh matrix) structures a choice among options by scoring each option against weighted criteria, replacing gut-feel comparison with explicit trade-offs. It is especially useful for multi-factor decisions like choosing a job, apartment, or major purchase, where different options win on different dimensions. This lesson teaches you to build and sanity-check one.", ["Paper or spreadsheet", "Pen or computer"], ["List your realistic options as rows and your decision criteria (cost, quality, time, fit) as columns", "Assign each criterion a weight from 1-5 reflecting its true importance to you", "Score each option on each criterion from 1-5, using evidence where possible", "Multiply scores by weights and sum each row to rank the options", "Sanity-check the winner: if the result feels wrong, your weights may not reflect your real priorities, which is itself valuable information"], "What is the purpose of assigning weights to criteria in a decision matrix?", "Weights reflect that criteria differ in importance, so more important factors influence the final score proportionally more than minor ones", "If the matrix's winner disappoints you, notice that feeling; it reveals a priority you didn't put in the weights, so update them and rerun."),
    ("Pre-Mortems: Failing in Advance", "A pre-mortem, a technique developed by research psychologist Gary Klein, asks a team to imagine that a plan has already failed spectacularly and then work backward to explain why. By making failure hypothetical-but-certain, it licenses people to voice doubts that optimism and group pressure normally suppress, surfacing risks while there is still time to fix them. This lesson teaches you to run pre-mortems for projects and personal decisions.", ["Notebook or whiteboard", "Pen", "Timer"], ["Before finalizing a plan, set the scene: 'It is one year from now and this project has failed completely'", "Set a timer and independently write down every plausible reason for the failure, without filtering", "If working with others, have everyone write privately first, then share, to avoid anchoring on the first idea", "Sort the failure causes by likelihood and impact", "For the top risks, add a prevention step or early-warning sign to the plan before launching"], "How does a pre-mortem differ from simply asking 'what could go wrong?'", "By assuming failure has already happened, it reframes the exercise from defending the plan to explaining a certain failure, which psychologically frees people to surface doubts that optimism and social pressure usually suppress", "Phrase it as 'It failed; why?' rather than 'Could it fail?'; the past tense is the ingredient that unlocks honest answers."),
    ("Red Teaming Your Own Ideas", "Red teaming, a practice originating in military exercises and now used in security and business, assigns a person or group to deliberately attack a plan, argument, or system in order to expose weaknesses before real adversaries or reality does. Applied to thinking, it means institutionalizing devil's advocacy: giving someone the explicit job of dismantling your idea, or doing it yourself in a structured way. This lesson teaches how to run a fair, useful red team exercise.", ["Notebook or whiteboard", "Pen", "A willing partner (optional)"], ["Write your plan or position with its supporting reasons as clearly as you can", "Assign a red team, a partner or yourself in an explicit adversary role, with the sole mission of defeating the idea", "Attack systematically: question each premise, stress-test each assumption, and hunt for scenarios where the plan breaks", "Record every attack that lands, without rebutting during the exercise", "Afterward, revise the plan to address the strongest attacks, or abandon it if a fatal flaw emerged"], "What is the purpose of red teaming a plan or argument?", "To find weaknesses by deliberately attacking it from an adversary's perspective before reality or opponents do, counteracting the natural tendency to defend rather than test one's own ideas", "Make the adversarial role explicit and time-boxed; criticism that's assigned as a job produces candor that polite feedback never will."),
    ("Probabilistic Forecasting and Calibration", "Calibration means that when you say you're 70% confident, you turn out to be right about 70% of the time, and research on forecasting, notably Philip Tetlock's Good Judgment Project, shows calibration is a trainable skill. Good forecasters replace vague words like 'probably' with explicit probabilities, break big questions into parts, start from base rates, and score themselves against outcomes. This lesson starts you on that practice.", ["Notebook or spreadsheet", "Pen"], ["Replace vague forecasts with numbers: not 'it'll probably ship on time' but 'I'm 75% confident it ships by March'", "Start each forecast from a base rate: how often do similar things happen in general?", "Record forecasts with dates and confidence levels in a log you cannot quietly edit", "When outcomes arrive, score yourself and look for patterns, such as chronic overconfidence at 90%", "Practice on ten small predictions over the coming month, from sports to project deadlines, and review your calibration"], "What does it mean to be well-calibrated as a forecaster?", "Your stated confidence matches your actual accuracy: claims made with 70% confidence come true about 70% of the time, and so on across confidence levels", "Most beginners discover their '90% sure' claims are right far less than 90% of the time; a written forecast log is the cheapest humility training available."),
    ("Ethical Reasoning Frameworks", "Three major ethical frameworks from philosophy offer complementary lenses on hard choices: consequentialism judges actions by their outcomes, deontology judges them by duties and rules regardless of outcomes, and virtue ethics asks what a person of good character would do. Used together as tools rather than dogmas, they surface considerations a single perspective misses. This lesson teaches you to apply all three lenses to real dilemmas.", ["Notebook", "Pen"], ["Learn consequentialism (associated with utilitarians like Jeremy Bentham and John Stuart Mill): which option produces the best overall outcomes?", "Learn deontology (associated with Immanuel Kant): what duties, rights, and rules apply, regardless of consequences? Would I will this as a universal rule?", "Learn virtue ethics (rooted in Aristotle): what would an honest, courageous, compassionate person do here? What does this choice make me?", "Apply all three lenses to one dilemma, such as whether to report a friend's wrongdoing, and note where they agree and conflict", "When the lenses conflict, weigh which considerations matter most in this context, and be able to say why"], "How do consequentialism and deontology differ in judging an action?", "Consequentialism judges an action by its outcomes, while deontology judges it by whether it conforms to moral duties and rules, which can condemn an action even when it produces good results", "When two ethical lenses agree, act with confidence; when they conflict, you've found what actually makes the dilemma hard, which is where careful thought belongs."),
    ("Disagreeing Productively", "Productive disagreement treats a dispute as a joint search for truth rather than a battle to win, using tools like steelmanning, identifying cruxes (the specific points that would change each side's mind), and separating factual disputes from value disputes. Techniques such as Rapoport's rules, popularized by philosopher Daniel Dennett, prescribe restating your opponent's position fairly and noting agreements before criticizing. This lesson turns earlier skills into a conversation method.", ["Notebook", "Pen", "A discussion partner (optional)"], ["Open with Rapoport's rules: restate the other view so fairly they say 'thanks, I wish I'd put it that way', list points of agreement, and mention what you learned from them, before any rebuttal", "Find the crux: ask 'What evidence would change your mind?' and answer the same question yourself", "Sort the disagreement: is it about facts (checkable), definitions (fixable), or values (negotiable trade-offs)?", "Argue toward the crux with evidence rather than repeating conclusions louder", "Close by summarizing what each side would need to see to update, even if no one changed their mind today"], "What is a 'crux' in a disagreement?", "A specific claim or piece of evidence such that, if you changed your mind about it, you would change your overall position; identifying cruxes focuses the discussion on what actually drives the disagreement", "If neither of you can name evidence that would change your mind, you're not having a factual disagreement; pause and figure out what kind of disagreement it actually is."),
    ("Capstone: Your Personal Thinking Checklist", "Pilots and surgeons use checklists because expertise alone doesn't prevent predictable errors under pressure, and the same logic applies to thinking: biases strike hardest exactly when you're rushed, emotional, or overconfident. This capstone lesson has you distill the whole pathway, from fallacies and biases to evidence standards and decision tools, into a short personal checklist you actually use. A one-page tool applied consistently beats a course's worth of knowledge applied never.", ["Notebook or index card", "Pen"], ["Review the pathway and list the five to ten thinking errors you are personally most prone to", "For each, write one short trigger question, such as 'What would change my mind?', 'Out of how many?', or 'Am I judging the argument or the person?'", "Add a standard evidence check: source credibility, sample, base rate, and whether the claim is correlation or causation", "Condense everything onto one page or card, and define when you'll use it: before sharing, before big decisions, during disagreements", "Test it on one real decision or claim this week, then revise the checklist based on what helped and what you skipped"], "Why is a short checklist more effective than simply knowing about biases and fallacies?", "Because biases operate automatically, especially under pressure, knowledge alone rarely prevents them; a checklist externalizes the checks into a routine you actually perform at the moment of judgment", "Keep the checklist short enough to actually use; five questions you always ask beat fifty you never do, and revise it as you learn which errors are really yours."),
]
